        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.michigan.gov/libraryofmichigan/public/michigan/county-guides/guides/mecosta",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Grand Rapids and Indiana Railroad was the first to reach Mecosta County, crossing the Little Muskegon River in June 1869 and sparking the village of Morley.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.mecostacounty.org/about_us/",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "A historic jail once stood where the current Old Historic Jail on Stewart Street was built in 1893.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.mecostacounty.org/about_us/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The State Legislature authorized Mecosta County in 1859 and made the Village of Leonard the county seat.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.mecostacounty.org/about_us/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "In 1883 county supervisors proposed a two‑year tax to fund a new courthouse, a key step in Mecosta’s civic development.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.mecostacounty.org/about_us/",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The East Branch of the Little Muskegon River winds through Mecosta, feeding the Muskegon River watershed that empties into Lake Michigan.",
